 "notes": "Defects of the original kept (behaviour unchanged, each commented in the code):\n- On LENGERR, LOG-LEN logs the START record's full original length (what CICS puts in LENGTH), not the\n  20 bytes actually moved into WS-DATA. A length of 10000 or more would also lose its high-order digit(s)\n  in PIC 9(4). Fix: MOVE FUNCTION MIN(WS-LEN 20) TO LOG-LEN.\n- Any RETRIEVE response other than NORMAL or LENGERR ends the loop quietly, as if it were end of data.\n  That includes INVREQ, IOERR and NOTFND, not only ENDDATA. Fix: set WS-MORE to 'N' only on\n  DFHRESP(ENDDATA) and abend on anything else.\n- LOG-RESP is PIC 99 unsigned, so it keeps only the absolute value mod 100 of EIBRESP. That is harmless\n  for the values RETRIEVE returns.\n\nFacts:\n- No fact is contradicted by the source. The uow_handlers RESP_CHECK (LENGERR, NORMAL at line 28) matches\n  lines 32-33.\n\nAssumptions:\n- runTask records WRITEQ TS through task.writeqTs, because the harness compares task events. The\n  generated writeqTsGtlogL41 (Spring TempStorage) is used only by executeGtwork.\n- executeGtwork has no START data, so it runs the loop once against ENDDATA. It writes the single record\n  \"R=29 L=0000 D=\" followed by 20 spaces.\n- The WS-LOG bytes are encoded in EBCDIC IBM037. The CSD and the facts do not name the region's code\n  page; if the region uses another EBCDIC page, change the EBCDIC constant.\n- The unsigned PIC 99 / 9(4) DISPLAY digits are written as plain zoned digits (F0-F9) through that\n  charset, without CobolRecords. These fields have no sign to overpunch.\n- The DFHRESP values used: NORMAL=0, NOTFND=13, INVREQ=16, IOERR=17, LENGERR=22, ENDDATA=29.",
